From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brennoflavio <contato@brennoflavio.com.br>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 01:44:16 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] webapi: mock Neo animation frames at native 60 Hz cadence

Replace the fixed 5 ms Window and dedicated-worker requestAnimationFrame
wait with per-global, phase-preserving 60 Hz deadlines for the Neo profile.
Quantize deadlines to the existing millisecond scheduler instead of adding
random timestamp noise or changing performance.now. Skip missed refreshes
rather than accumulating a fixed 17 ms delay or running catch-up callbacks.

Share one native performance timestamp among callbacks registered for the
same pending frame. Detach that frame at first dispatch so callback and
microtask registrations target the next frame. Refcount the shared frame
through the existing callback completion, cancellation and task-finalizer
paths; retain native bridge shape, IDs and exception/microtask handling.

Allow scheduler tasks to retain an absolute deadline so all callbacks in a
frame keep registration order even across clock-tick/preemption boundaries.
Relative timeout, interval and idle scheduling remains unchanged. Reuse the
existing per-callback tasks and runner completion accounting, not a new
compositor or a global clock patch.

Scope: a synthetic foreground display cadence, not graphics rendering,
visibility throttling, actual vsync, ProMotion or adaptive refresh. The
millisecond quantization is observable and is not a claim of exact Chrome
timing. Keep iframe/worker loading defaults and benchmark assertions intact.

References:
https://www.apple.com/macbook-neo/specs/
https://hothardware.com/reviews/apple-macbook-neo-review
https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/imagebitmap-and-animations.html#animation-frames

Validation before export: existing upstream tests 1658/1658; temporary
Window/worker CDP diagnostics 35/35, including 2000-callback ordering,
cancellation, microtasks, nested frames and pending navigation/worker
teardown under allocation pressure. No new persistent unit tests.
Two unchanged baseline benchmark runs: 86/111 (77.48%), up from 85/111;
frame-cadence is the only status change. One reviewer pass completed;
absolute-deadline ordering finding corrected and validation rerun.
